+## Dossier Research and Review
+
+Start an authorized dossier review with the client, supplied document, approved governing version and [pilot boundary](product.md#purpose-and-scope). Read the whole dossier and existing comments and suggestions. Identify its explicit unknowns and requests for confirmation, consolidate repeated requests about the same fact, and distinguish them from statements presented as fact. Use these questions to scope client research; dossier wording does not establish an answer.
+
+Reuse the source-discovery and investigation methods in [Understand the Practice](#understand-the-practice): examine relevant navigation and sitemap entries, follow service and clinician links, combine complementary evidence and search for unanswered questions. Use practice sources for the practice's own services and care; use authoritative primary sources suited to other questions, such as credentials or general treatment facts. Read the source passages and their context. Search snippets, repeated marketing claims and source recency alone do not establish an answer. Do not send nonpublic dossier details to public search services. This operation does not require an article assignment, competitor-content review, outline or draft.
+
+Compare the research results with each reviewed unknown. Suggest only what the evidence establishes for the correct client, clinician, product, location and time; preserve material qualifications. A general product description does not establish the practice's installed model, current availability or a clinician's preference. Stop expanding a question when it has an adequately supported answer, further relevant pages repeat the evidence, or targeted searches yield no further usable evidence. Record unsupported or inconclusive answers and required client confirmation under [Data Model](data-model.md#dossiers-and-review-evidence). Record factual conflicts encountered for later review; do not turn them into conflict comments during this pilot. Missing public information does not disprove an internal fact.
+
+Prepare the supported answer and its [source comment](userdesign.md#dossier-review) before posting. Recheck the target text and existing review work immediately before each write; reconcile changes rather than overwriting them or duplicating a suggestion. Preserve the original document, sharing and other reviewers' work. Confirm that the actual file supports Suggesting mode and comments; a Word file opened in Google Docs need not be converted if these work. If conversion or a different document is needed, bring the concrete choice to Vikas before replacing the authoritative target. Never substitute direct text edits for suggestions.
+
+For the first pilot, post one supported answer as a real suggestion with its source comment, then verify it under [Engineering](engineering.md#dossier-review-verification) before posting the remaining prepared answers. If either interaction fails or is uncertain, inspect the current document before retrying and report the blocker; do not create test filler or duplicate review items. Complete the evidence-backed assessment of research coverage and limits even if posting is blocked. Record later replies or text changes when reviewing Peter's feedback; posting alone does not establish his acceptance or that the process is useful to him. Do not schedule follow-up monitoring unless requested.
